# resolution_tripwire - ONE KNOWN-WRONG BEHAVIOR, PINNED SO IT CANNOT MOVE
#                       SILENTLY.  Read tests/tripwire_test.cryo first.
#
# A `private` function is callable from another module. Spec §3.3 says
# "enforcement is a gate"; today visibility is only a tie-breaker, so a
# declaration is reachable from anywhere as long as it is unambiguous.
#
# THE FLIP PROTOCOL. When the visibility gate lands this test STARTS FAILING.
# That is the design. Convert this project to `compile_fail` with
# `expect.diagnostic` E0353 in the SAME change that makes it an error -- do
# not adjust the assertion to get back to green.
#
# WHY THIS IS STILL HERE (measured 2026-08-04, and it cost a full attempt).
# The fix looks separable from the §5.2 keystone and is NOT. There are three
# doors -- a written qualified path, the bare binder's single-overload fast
# path, and the multi-candidate path via `bare_candidate_scope` -- and all
# three were shut, with the compiler and stdlib still self-building clean.
# It still had to be reverted: every gate needs a "does the use site live in
# the declaring module?" exemption, and that question is answered by
# `current_module_ns()`, which is the AMBIENT CURSOR.
#
# The cursor is wrong often enough to break the build. `std::fmt::display`'s
# `private fmt_err` is called ONLY from its own file (display.cryo:610-656,
# no cross-module caller exists), yet the gate rejected 8 of those calls
# claiming the use site was `std::core::result` -- the f-string lowering
# synthesizes those calls and they are checked with the cursor parked
# somewhere else entirely.
#
# So visibility enforcement is blocked on the keystone supplying a real
# scope, exactly as spec §8.1d states. The counter's `FnBindPrivateBound == 0`
# reads like evidence the gate is safe to switch on; it is not, because the
# failure mode is FALSE POSITIVES from a wrong cursor, which that counter
# does not measure.
#
# Its sibling defect - a path silently dropping TRAILING module segments -
# WAS fixed on 2026-08-04 and is now pinned as a real compile-fail case at
# tests/tests/negative/E0203_path_drops_trailing_segment.cryo.

